1: # Actual signed returns for the one-cap additive families
2: 
3: P3K.26 proof submitted for root audit, 2026-11-17. This applies to
4: the actual constructions of P3F.37 and the complementary starred,
5: ordinary III/IV and II caps of F41--43. Their sole logarithmic
6: norm is \(n=1\) or \(n=-1\). The actual parameter is
7: \(\lambda=r^m\), with one positive loop in the punctured
8: \(r\)-disc. Both norm signs have the same oriented return
9: integer in the table below. The proof retains the arrows, source
10: line diagrams, and normal Thom orientation for each sign.
11: 
12: ## AR.1. Physical characters and the complete parameter square
13: 
14: Move the capped source value to infinity and denote the selected
15: scalar-zero nodal value by \(t_c\). Near that cusp write
16: \(q=t-t_c\), \(s=\log q/(2\pi i)\), and
17: \(v=\log r/(2\pi i)\). In physical characters the source
18: Abel coordinate is \(a=\zeta_1\) and the scalar line coordinate
19: is \(V=e^{2\pi i\zeta_2}\). Let \(g\) be the marked Abel
20: logarithm of \(Q=bG\). The actual suspension map is
21: \[
22:  B_\lambda(a,V)=(a-g,e^{2\pi iC}qr^mV),\qquad
23:  \gamma_{\rm out}=(g,-C-s-mv),\qquad \delta=(0,1).
24:                                                         \tag{AR.1}
25: \]
26: Thus \(B_\lambda\) is translation by \(-\gamma_{\rm out}\),
27: and \(\gamma_{\rm out}=B_\lambda^{-1}\) is exactly the
28: outward loop used in the cap meridian. Delta is positive physical
29: line rotation. The functions \(g,C\) have bounded holomorphic
30: characters in the specified nodal chart; their constants and
31: any fixed line character are retained.
32: 
33: The annular arrow is \(F_c:\text{ordinary}\to\text{cap}\),
34: where \(c=\log\lambda/(2\pi i)\). Its scalar variation is
35: \(n(c-c_0)s_\infty\delta/m\), as follows directly from
36: the positive \(A_r\) root cut. At \(c\mapsto c+m\) the
37: cap is identical. The inverse closing \(c+m\to c\) is
38: \(F_c^{-1}F_{c+m}\), whose global multiplier is
39: \((t-t_c)^{-n}\). Its positive deck is consequently
40: \[
41:               (v,\zeta)\longmapsto(v+1,\zeta+n s\delta).
42:                                                         \tag{AR.2}
43: \]
44: This is fixed by the actual ordinary-to-cap arrow, including its
45: base unit; it is not deduced from a later orientation convention.
46: 
47: On the auxiliary cusp cover \(q=Q_0^{2m}\), use the two
48: extending origins
49: \[
50: \begin{split}
51:  o_n(s,v)&=n\bigl(-gs/m,\ Cs/m+s^2/(2m)+sv\bigr),\\
52:  \sigma_n(s,v)&=n\bigl(gv,\ -Cv-m(v^2-v)/2\bigr).
53: \end{split}                                             \tag{AR.3}
54: \]
55: They satisfy the exact identities
56: \[
57: \begin{split}
58:  o_n(s,v+1)-o_n(s,v)&=n s\delta,\\
59:  \sigma_n(s,v+1)-\sigma_n(s,v)&=n s\delta+n\gamma_{\rm out},\\
60:  o_n(s+2m,v)-o_n(s,v)&=-2n\gamma_{\rm out}+2mn\delta,\\
61:  \sigma_n(s+2m,v)-\sigma_n(s,v)&=0.
62: \end{split}                                             \tag{AR.4}
63: \]
64: The characters of \(\sigma_n\) are bounded units for either
65: sign, so they extend in the actual Mumford charts. The ordinary
66: origin removes (AR.2). On their common punctured collar their
67: difference is the explicit translation
68: \[
69:  F_n=\sigma_n-o_n
70:  =n\left(g(s/m+v),-C(s/m+v)-\frac{(s+mv)^2}{2m}+\frac{mv}{2}\right).
71: \]
72: Its angular change is
73: \[
74:  F_n(s,v+\theta)-F_n(s,v)
75:    =n\theta\gamma_{\rm out}
76:                    -\frac{nm}{2}\theta(\theta-1)\delta.  \tag{AR.5}
77: \]
78: The quadratic line gauge vanishes at both endpoints. The whole
79: displayed difference changes under \(s\mapsto s+2m\) by
80: \(-2mn\theta\delta\), an integral retained line winding.
81: On the orbit the actual positive \(r\)-loop is therefore
82: \(n\gamma_{\rm out}\). The auxiliary root is in the cusp
83: base and does not replace this loop by a parameter cover.
84: 
85: Changing the root of the norm unit by \(\zeta_m^d\) adds a
86: fixed delta character to the cap. Its actual graph line is still
87: the norm sphere of \(N\); that character is retained in the
88: sheet identifications. It is independent of \(v\), so it cancels
89: in (AR.2)--(AR.5). The affine and line conjugations of F44 are
90: also independent of \(r\), preserve \(B_0\), and transport
91: the annular map. Thus the same identities apply after those
92: exact local comparisons, without dropping their boundary gauges.
93: 
94: ## AR.2. The signed Seifert map and the actual complex page
95: 
96: Use the actual root coordinate \(q_\infty=z^m\) and the
97: cap norm coordinate
98: \[
99:  u=m\gamma_{\rm out}-n\frac{\arg q_\infty}{2\pi}.
100: \]
101: Its quotient relation is
102: \((z,u)\sim(\zeta_mz,u-n)\), with \(u\) of period \(m\).
103: For both signs let \(R>0\) solve
104: \(R^2+|z|^2R^{2/m}=1\). The actual map is
105: \[
106:  (z,u)\longmapsto(z_1,z_2)=
107:  \left(zR^{1/m}e^{2\pi i n u/m},R e^{2\pi i n u}\right).
108:                                                         \tag{AR.6}
109: \]
110: It is invariant under the quotient relation because \(n^2=1\).
111: The positive derivative of the radial equation and the inverse
112: \(R=|z_2|\), followed by a lift of its argument and
113: \(z=z_1R^{-1/m}e^{-2\pi inu/m}\), prove that it is a smooth
114: marked solid-torus diffeomorphism. Changing the lift is exactly
115: a power of the original root action. Its base projection is
116: \[
117:  t=z_2/z_1^m=q_\infty^{-1}.
118: \]
119: On the ordinary base disc the same map is
120: \(z_1=\rho e^{2\pi in\gamma_{\rm out}}\),
121: \(z_2=t z_1^m\), with the unique positive \(\rho\) satisfying
122: \(\rho^2+|t|^2\rho^{2m}=1\). The two displayed maps agree
123: on the overlap by the exact equation for \(u\). They identify
124: the actual three-dimensional orbit-base gluing with \(S^3\),
125: not merely its abstract Seifert invariants.
126: 
127: The positive weighted Hopf coordinate is
128: \[
129:                          \beta=n\gamma_{\rm out}.       \tag{AR.7}
130: \]
131: Choose a regular binding value \(\tau\). The affine complex
132: page \(b-\tau a^m=1\) embeds in this sphere by
133: \((a,b)\mapsto(\rho a,\rho^m b)\), where \(\rho>0\)
134: puts it on the sphere. Its projection is
135: \[
136:                          t=\tau+a^{-m}.                \tag{AR.8}
137: \]
138: The compactified page base is \(\mathbb P^1_a\). It is
139: ramified to order \(m\) at the additive cap and at the
140: regular binding, and it is unramified at the selected nodal
141: cusp and at every other source singular fibre. In particular
142: the selected cusp contributes \(m\) separate \(I_1\) fibres.
143: 
144: The root-chart page equation is
145: \[
146:  n u+\frac{\arg(1-\tau z^m)}{2\pi}=\frac\phi{2\pi}
147:                                   \pmod{\mathbb Z}.     \tag{AR.9}
148: \]
149: Its \(m\) sheets are cyclically permuted by the actual
150: \(A_r\), which decreases the sheet index by one for either
151: norm sign. Choosing sheet zero gives the exact source-root
152: slice. For phase zero the page coordinates satisfy
153: \[
154:  a^m=\frac{z^m}{1-\tau z^m},\qquad
155:  b=\frac1{1-\tau z^m}.                                 \tag{AR.10}
156: \]
157: These are holomorphic, independent of \(n\). Thus the page
158: inherits the actual complex source orientation for both signs.
159: 
160: ## AR.3. The actual root line, finite-base torsor, and square
161: 
162: On the cap, the sheet-zero map is
163: \(p\mapsto[p,u_0(z(p))]\) on the complete resolved root
164: surface, including its exceptional components and tangencies.
165: Every root sheet has a unique representative in this one; no
166: residual finite stabilizer quotient remains. On the circle line,
167: normalize a nonzero vector in \(N_p\) to have the indicated
168: norm coordinate. This is an explicit equivariant map from the
169: norm sphere of \(N\) to the actual delta bundle on the page.
170: It uses the resolved line identities of F41--43, including the
171: good-starred toric corrections, the good III/IV exceptional
172: differences, and the cancelling II correction. It therefore
173: identifies the actual line, not only its generic Picard point.
174: 
175: On the ordinary finite-base charts the page is the unramified
176: source pullback. At the selected nodal pieces the actual
177: circle-line/orbit charts of P3F.9 give the same source Picard
178: line, with the conifold Hopf charge retained in the later
179: compression. The page phase and the regular-binding bands in
180: P3K.6 and P3F.31 are chosen using (AR.7); since its positive
181: parameter angle is \(\beta\) for both signs, their exact
182: avoidance and line-carrying band maps apply unchanged. The
183: finite-base page is consequently a source translation torsor
184: with the specified line and the marked binding collar.
185: 
186: Here is its comparison with the sectioned compact page, relative
187: to that collar. The complement of the capped source fibre is
188: simply connected in F41--43. The section kills the base loops,
189: so its vanishing cycles span the full integral elliptic lattice
190: \(\Lambda\). Lifts of these cycles in one sector of (AR.8)
191: retain that span. Thus the monodromy coinvariant group on the
192: page base with binding boundary is zero. For the constructible
193: lattice of periods,
194: \[
195:  H^2(C,\partial C;j_*\Lambda)=0.                       \tag{AR.11}
196: \]
197: Indeed compact-support duality on the punctured oriented base
198: identifies the corresponding \(j_!\)-group with the coinvariant
199: lattice. The quotient \(j_*\Lambda/j_!\Lambda\) is a finite
200: skyscraper and has no first cohomology. This proves (AR.11)
201: with the actual boundary marking. The component quotient of the
202: extending translation sheaf is also a skyscraper and has no
203: relative first cohomology. After removing it, lift the remaining
204: translation cocycle to the smooth Lie sheaf. Its period
205: two-cocycle vanishes by (AR.11), and a partition of unity that
206: vanishes on the prescribed collar kills the remaining Lie
207: cocycle. Exponentiation gives the actual smooth translation
208: isomorphism with the sectioned page, fixed on that collar.
209: 
210: The line comparison under these smooth base translations is
211: made through the rigidified fibrewise Picard map, rather than
212: an unproved holomorphic divisor for a smooth total-space map.
213: On a local holomorphic translation chart form
214: \(\operatorname{Hom}(N,T^*N)\). Its restriction is trivial on
215: every fibre. At a smooth fibre this is the constant-factor
216: degree-zero uniformization. At a reduced additive fibre the
217: component degrees are zero; the proper direct-image generator
218: has vertical divisor in the whole-fibre kernel, so division by
219: that base monomial gives a nowhere-zero section. At a node it
220: is the normalization gluing unit, preserved by translation.
221: Thus the fibre \(h^0\) is one everywhere, and proper base
222: change plus evaluation gives a holomorphic line isomorphism
223: after pulling out a base line. Rigidify it along the zero
224: section, then pull back by the actual smooth translation
225: section. This is the desired smooth line isomorphism.
226: 
227: On the compactification its remaining base ambiguity is an
228: integral fibre class \(kF\). The actual line is therefore
229: compared, for its square, with
230: \[
231:                          M_{\rm page}=P_{aG'}-O.
232: \]
233: Here \(M_{\rm page}F=F^2=0\), so adding \(kF\) changes
234: neither its square nor the characteristic pairings. On the
235: regular binding collar the line extends over the actual
236: \(T^2\)-bundle over a disc and has fibre degree zero; its
237: boundary first Chern class is zero. The relative boundary-gauge
238: ambiguity consequently has zero pairing with the line and
239: with itself, by the exact relative product calculation P3K.5.
240: This retains even an odd boundary gauge.
241: 
242: ## AR.4. The Thom sign for each physical return
243: 
244: By (AR.5) and (AR.7), the positive physical parameter coordinate
245: \(\theta\) is the positive weighted coordinate \(\beta\)
246: for both signs of \(n\). The complex boundary of the affine
247: page has positive \(\arg a\), hence
248: \[
249:                          \partial V=+E\times S^1_\theta.
250: \]
251: Orient the actual compression by \(\partial W=F-E\).
252: Attaching its rotated product gives the trace with
253: \(\partial X=+F\times S^1_\theta\). In the actual
254: fixed-sphere Hopf chart the ambient orientation is
255: \[
256:  {\rm or}(F)\,{\rm or}(\nu_{\rm Hopf})\,dr\,d\theta,
257: \]
258: whereas the trace tangent is \({\rm or}(F)\,dr\,d\theta\).
259: Moving the three normal directions past the two last directions
260: has positive sign. Thus the induced normal three-plane is the
261: positive Hopf model and the actual Euler line satisfies
262: \(\pi_*x=+1\) for each norm sign. The possible reversal of
263: the ambient source-product orientation in the signed \(S^3\)
264: identification is already included in this normal comparison;
265: it supplies no additional sign. This is the full signed
266: calculation of notes/part3_signed_cap_page.md, independently
267: executed in its sign certificate.
268: 
269: The two outer-bordism/Jin reversals are exactly those in P3K.7.
270: Consequently the actual positive-return invariant is
271: \[
272:  A^{\rm Jin}=\frac{12M_{\rm page}^2-p_1(TS_{\rm page})}{24}.
273:                                                         \tag{AR.12}
274: \]
275: Both the circle line and its orientation have now been compared
276: to the actual page, so this equation applies to the constructed
277: return rather than to a formal characteristic-number model.
278: 
279: ## AR.5. Exact arithmetic and the oriented classes
280: 
281: Let \(e\) be the original capped Kodaira Euler number and
282: \(e'\) the resolved root Euler number. The compact Jacobian
283: page in (AR.8) has
284: \[
285:  \chi=\frac{m(12-e)+e'}{12},\quad
286:  \sigma=-8\chi,\quad p_1=-24\chi.                    \tag{AR.13}
287: \]
288: All other source fibres are unramified, and the binding is
289: regular, so the displayed Euler count includes every fibre.
290: The actual line point meets the identity component at every
291: retained reducible fibre and at each reduced additive root.
292: Good roots are irreducible. The height base-change formula
293: therefore gives, with \(h(G)=1/d\),
294: \[
295:  h(aG')=\frac{ma^2}{d}=2\chi+2(P_{aG'}\cdot O),
296:  \qquad M_{\rm page}^2=-\frac{ma^2}{d}.
297: \]
298: Combining this actual line comparison with (AR.12) yields
299: \[
300:  A^{\rm Jin}=\chi-\frac{ma^2}{2d}=-P_{aG'}\cdot O.
301:                                                         \tag{AR.14}
302: \]
